 approval ratings for an unknown were  overwhelmingly high  and part of the reason was just the  physical contrast he made  with boris yeltsin by 1999 most russians  were ready for a change  the hero who stood on the tank in 1991  and broke communist control over freedom  of speech  elections and private property was also  blamed by those  who saw their life savings disappear as  a new class of billionaires  scooped up state factories yeltsin's  lengthy disappearances from public view  were blamed on heart attacks  or excessive drinking to many russians  president boris yeltsin felt like an  embarrassment  he was very famous for so this is all  the build-up to get to the great putin  right this is all about the bad stuff  about yeltsin first  he inibriated he was a non-alcoholic and  a big drinker  he was very large um just a stature  so the way that he behaved in these  high-level  public diplomacy meetings through the  u.s presidents or other heads of state  embarrassed a lot of russians at the  time the physical contrast with  putin was stark and if you're if you're  not watching  they they're running video of putin  lifting weights and then it gets even  more sycophantic  he's a martial arts champion he says he  never drinks  and for many russians that also gave  them a sense of stability  mr putin embodied that physically in a  way  that president yeltsin absolutely did  not  putin also embodied a return to an  authoritarian past  shutting down press freedom banishing  independent oligarchs  and consolidating power in his own hands  putin could be around for a long time to  come he's changed the constitution he  could potentially  be in office until the year 2036  john he knows how to consolidate power  no question about that  steve harrigan for steve thank you so  much and  and can you imagine sandra boris yeltsin  on horseback without a shirt on  not a pretty picture yeah so there it is  and so fundamentally what  what i think is going on here there are  more and more people that are actually  calling out  not just the right but also those on the  left who are playing coy  with these guys um i i think there's a  couple  important things to uh to to sort of  keep in mind here  uh number one is that um there  is no circumstance in which progressive  values  align with the way that vladimir putin  runs  government uh bashar al-assad  nicholas maduro sometimes there will be  people on the left even even defending  hamas it's often the exact same people  um  there there's nothing progressive about  that and it's really important to  to bear that in mind but one of the  really  toxic things that's going on with the  left on this issue  is when someone like me for example  calls this out  i will be called everything from a  zionist even though this doesn't  directly relate  putin doesn't directly relate to the  israeli-palestinian conflict although  some of those people are also playing  pretty koi  with hamas to a neo-liberal  colonialist to a faux progressive  to a right-winger etc etc but  what i would challenge those folks to  look at is  if you look at the history of social  democracy around the world  and in northern europe etc when has  progressive social democracy ever looked  like people  like vladimir putin and assad  and she and maduro etc  and so the case i would make is that  this sort of authoritarian left that  plays  coy with this stuff that fox news does  on putin and others  they are the ones that have been either  led astray or for whatever personal  reasons  have decided that that's the direction  they want to go in  the progressive social democracy  movement looks quite similar today to  what it did decades and decades ago  and unfortunately with a lot of these  examples  if you follow the money you start to  learn a lot about why some of these  factions are staking out the positions  that they're staking out and maybe we'll  say more about that in the future  i'm really excited that one of our  sponsors t